From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Luke Craig <lacraig3@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 28 Oct 2026 14:58:36 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] plugins: emit block completion at outgoing edges

Add a translation-time TB-end callback for normal completion. Emit it after outgoing CPU-state updates and before direct/indirect links or dispatcher returns; for an indirect link, before the target lookup, whose result the link takes in the same extended basic block. Move direct-link opcodes past their fallback PC updates only for instrumented translations. Faults, nonlocal exits and entry rejection do not report completion.

End callbacks run after the PC has moved on. A CF_PCREL TB that has them stays shared between the virtual addresses that map it: at its start it stores the address it runs at in CPUState.plugin_tb_vaddr (TCGCPUOps.gen_tb_pc, implemented for x86, arm and riscv), where the embedding finds it. A target without gen_tb_pc binds such a TB to its address instead.

Add gated TB-entry and TB-end callbacks. The gate is a byte at a fixed offset from env (CPUState.plugin_tb_gates), so the embedding turns the callbacks on and off for every vCPU without retranslation and without a scoreboard index. The gate byte and the address take two CPUState fields that were kept unused for the layout. plugin_cb_flags is set around a gated call as around any callback, but the call itself syncs no registers (TCG_CALL_NO_RWG): every global is in env before the gate, and no register copy of one is used after it.

On hosts whose backend implements it (x86_64), a gated callback is one TCG op, plugin_gate, so the gate ends no basic block. Liveness treats it as an op with side effects (globals synced before it), the optimizer forgets what it knows of globals and memory at it, and the register allocator drops the register copies of globals at it and has the backend preserve the call-clobbered registers that hold temps around the call. The backend tests the gate byte inline and places the call by the gate's state at translation: inline, behind a forward branch, if the gate is open, else after the block's code, behind a branch taken when the gate is open, returning to the code after the test. Targets with indirect globals, and other hosts, load the byte, test the callback's bits and branch around the call.

Remove deferred PANDA completion delivery and pending-block reads from the CPU loop.

